#lang racket

;; rope/benchmarks/sequence-bench.rkt
;;
;; Times a full left-to-right traversal via in-string-rope / in-string-cursor
;; (the per-type sequence wrappers generated by define-rope-sequence /
;; define-cursor-sequence), written literally inside a for clause so the
;; compile-time-specialized :do-in fast path is what actually gets measured
;; -- not the make-do-sequence fallback. A raw in-string traversal of an
;; equivalent plain Racket string is included as a reference point for how
;; much overhead the cursor machinery adds on top of a bare loop.
;;
;; Currently, cursor-advance! is called once per *element* even while
;; walking within a single leaf, where a plain index increment and a direct
;; chunk-ref would do. Run this once now for a "before" baseline, then again
;; after changing the iterators to only touch the cursor once per leaf
;; crossing, to see the effect directly.

